Among the evil quintet, habitat loss is the prime cause of biodiversity loss globally as shown in the figure below. Habitat loss is the consequence of several drivers, but the most important driver is conversion of natural ecosystems into agricultural land. These conversions are very active in South America, Sub-Saharan Africa and South-East Asia, while they occurred in the past in Europe, North America and other parts of Asia.

Overview of main threats and their frequency for 703 declining terrestrial populations. Habitat loss accounts for 50% of the threats, overexploitation for 25%, Invasive species for 10% and climate change and pollution each for 7 to 8%

Agriculture now already occupies 51M km2, which is 50% of all inhabitable terrestrial area. Important to notice is that more than 75% of that agricultural area is used for livestock (grazing or feed crops), which may result in energetically less efficient food pathways and therefore more land occupation for food production than strictly necessary (see module Food security).

The loss of forest cover continues at a dazzling speed of about 20 million hectare per year. For the period 2001-2018 it resulted in a gross loss of forest cover of 3,6 M km2 (360 million ha). Gross because half of this cover loss recovers as secondary forest or regeneration after cut or fire. The other half, however, is converted to agriculture, of which about half in permanent agriculture and the other half in shifting cultivation.

