=== Overexploitation <sec:overexploitation>
Overexploitation is another important cause of biodiversity loss. In principle, plant and animal populations are renewable resources. It means that a harvesting level below the natural population growth would not harm the population size. It often implies intensive monitoring and strong governance rules to install such a system of sustainable use in a successful way. Examples are known from sustainable fisheries, game hunting, natural forest exploitation, etc.

But often the governance conditions are weak and overexploitation happens at the expense of the wildlife populations. This is the fate for many larger vertebrates, such as bison, whales, beavers, dodos, rhinos,… , which are or have been attractive as a resource of meat, fat, fur, horn, etc. for humans.

For the 362 megafauna species still occurring on this planet (they are mammals and fish of >100kg and birds, reptiles or amphibians of >40kg), overexploitation is the major factor of threat as you can see in the figure below.

#figure(
  image("assets/Overexplotatie-in-verschillende-diersoorten.png", width: 5cm),
  caption: [Overexplotatie-in-verschillende-diersoorten],
  label: <fig:Overexplotatie-in-verschillende-diersoorten>,
)
An example of overexploitation is the bushmeat market strongly developed in West Africa, that tends to turn tropical forests in ‘silent’ forests as a consequence of the absence of large animals. Among the traded species, all 8 species of pangolin worldwide are threatened from overexploitation. A side risk of the bushmeat and traditional medicine market is the transfer of zoonotic diseases from animals to humans. In the case of pangolins, it has been proposed that they might have been the intermediate host of Covid19 from bats to humans (Liu et al. 2020).

=== Invasive soorten <sec:invasive-species>

What are invasive alien species?

Alien (or exotic or non-native) species are species that are introduced by humans, accidentally or intentionally, outside of their natural geographic range into an area where they were not naturally present. This as opposed to indigenous (or native) species. A species is indigenous to a given region or ecosystem if its presence in that region is the result of only natural processes, with no human intervention.

Invasive alien species (IAS) are non-native species that cause economic or environmental harm or adversely affect human health. It is important to note that not all alien species cause harm and are thus classified as invasive.

How are alien species introduced?

