==== Bedoelde introducties <sec:bedoelde-introducties>

There are many reasons why people brought (and still bring) alien species into a country:

Ornamental plants: Many plants are imported for their ornamental value for gardens, parks, etc... An example of a very invasive species is the Japanese knotweed.
Beekeeping: for example Himalayan balsem was imported for the high nectar productivity, which is interesting for beekeeping.
Pets: often animals are imported as pets, but then escape or are released into the wild, for example turtles that grow too large.
Forestry: some plants were introduced as an interesting species for forestry, but then became invasive, such as the Black cherry
Pest control: for example the Asian lady beetle was introduced to control pests in greenhouses


==== Impact of invasive alien species <sec:impact-of-invasive-alien-species>

*Natuur*

Predation: e.g. the Asian lady beetle, which outcompetes and even eats other native lady beetles, or the American bull frog, which eats small birds, other amphibian species, fish, snails, insects, ...
Introduction of new diseases and pests: e.g. the Horse-chestnut leaf miners are an invasive pest of horse chestnut leading to an early autumn browing, or the Varrao mite parasites on bees, which carry around several viruses that can contribute to the 'colony collapse disorder'.
Competition with native species: e.g. Japanese knotweed, which has such a dominant growth it blocks the sunlight for other species, or the Himalayan balsam, which is a strong competitor for pollinators.

*Mensen*
Invasive species can also effect human health by many pathways. Here are some examples:

The tiger mosquito is a vector of many viral pathogens, including the yellow fever virus and dengue fever.
The ragweed Ambrosia artemisiifolia has strong allergenic properties and is invasive in Europe.
Invasive alien species can pose a threat to agriculture and forestry, e.g. the Giant African snail introduced to North and South America.


=== Vervuiling <sec:vervuiling>

Pollution is a general word for all substances that come into our natural environment causing it harm. This makes pollution a multifaceted problem, with many different forms and effects. All forms of pollution pose a (serious) threat to biodiversity. Some examples are:

Toxic chemicals, heavy metals from mining, industrial activities, …
Acid rain: Emissions of SO2 and NOx have an acidifying effect on precipitation
Plastic soup,
Nutrient loadings (N, P), ….


#figure(
  image("assets/Alle-soorten-vormen-van-vervuiling.png", width: 5cm),
  caption: [Alle-soorten-vormen-van-vervuiling],
  label: <fig:Alle-soorten-vormen-van-vervuiling>,
)

NITROGEN POLLUTION

Since pollution is such a diverse problem, we will look deeper into only one example: nitrogen deposition. Nitrogen pollution has different causes :

Exessive use of fertilizers on agricultural fields
Livestock production causes the emission of ammonia (NH3)
Burning of fossil fuels causes the emission of nitrogen oxides (NOx). NOx is a group of molecules consisting of nitrogen and oxygen atoms.

While the nitrogen in fertilizers is deposited locally, ammonia (NH3) and nitrogen oxides (NOx) are gases and can drift away and get deposited beyond the source site. We call this atmospheric deposition.

Many studies find negative effects of nitrogen depositon (NOx & NH3) on plant species richness. There are two main mechanisms in which nitrogen can have this negative effect:

Nitrogen is a key limiting nutrient for plant growth and thus stimulates plant growth. Although this sounds like a good thing at first, it’s actually not good news for many species. Most plant species are adapted to low nitrogen conditions, as these were predominant before the use of fossil fuels and intensive agricultural practices. A high nitrogen availability will favor a few competitive species instead of the many typical plant species, which become outcompeted by competitive species (like grasses, stinging nettles, brambles).
Nitrogen deposition results in acidification of the soil. Acidification has many impact pathways on biodiversity. One such pathway is that when a soil becomes more acid, protons and aluminium are released, which are toxic compounds for many plant species.


=== Klimaat verandering <sec:climate-change>

Klimaatverandering wordt groot besproken in het vorige hoofdstuk #ref(<ch:climate>).

In the last decades, global temperatures have been rising due to climate change. Important to note is that this warming is not uniform across the globe, the northern hemisphere appears to be warming more rapidly. Besides global temperature increase, we also see an increase in weather extremes, such as droughts, heat waves and heavy precipitation events. More information on the causes and effects of climate change can be found in the module on Climate.

==== Effect op biodiversiteit <sec:effect-op-biodiversiteit>

Changing climatic conditions cause shifts in climatically suitable areas and can result in habitat loss. The impact of this depends on the species.

Mobile species can move to new climatically suitable areas by migration (polewards or higher in elevation), and are therefore less affected. Still they also are impacted. For example for the Buff-tailed bumblebee, as you can see in the figure below, the loss of suitable habitat in the future (red) is higher than the gain in newly suitable habitat (green).


#figure(
  image("assets/Effect-van-klimaat-op-Buff-tailed-bumblebee.png", width: 5cm),
  caption: [Effect-van-klimaat-op-Buff-tailed-bumblebee],
  label: <fig:Effect-van-klimaat-op-Buff-tailed-bumblebee>,
)


Less mobile species or sessile species will be impacted more. Coral reefs are a good example. Corals are sessile invertebrate species that live in symbiosis with algae species. When the water becomes too warm, corals will expel the algae living in their tissues causing the coral to turn completely white as seen on the left side of the image below.


#figure(
  image("assets/Koraalriffen.png", width: 5cm),
  caption: [Koraalriffen],
  label: <fig:Koraalriffen>,
)

Another effect that climate change can have on species diversity is through a temporal mismatch in food webs. Species in food webs are evolved together and are attuned. For example the caterpillars feed on the fresh leaves of oaks, and when the eggs of the great tit hatch, the baby birds are fed on caterpillars. So it’s important that all these events happen at the same time. However, climate change can affect the timing of these events, and this effect can differ among species. When that happens, we have a temporal mismatch. In the case of the caterpillars, it has been observed that they peak much earlier in the season, so that when the eggs of the great tit hatch (which has also become more early in the season, but not as much as for the caterpillars), there are less caterpillars available for them to eat.

